WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.760
Have you ever wondered, ‘what is the best
number of legs?’

00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:08.280
I mean, probably not.

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:14.040
But how many legs a species has is vital to
a lifeform’s success, and it’s time someone

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:16.140
figured out which number reigns supreme.

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:22.380
So, for this entry into the archive, we’ll
explore every option for leg-count that nature

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:26.820
makes available, excluding cases of genetic
mutation or limb-loss.

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:30.120
Whether you’re reincarnating&nbsp;
soon or a worldbuilder&nbsp;

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:32.580
looking to improve the realism of an alien

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:35.640
lifeform, this is a question you need answered…

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:40.260
We’ll kick things off by discussing lifeforms
with one leg.

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:45.720
You might not think any species has a single
leg as their default setting, but nature doesn’t

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:46.560
care what you think.

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:51.360
Many clams and oysters use a single muscular
limb to drag themselves along.

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:57.240
And their gastropod relatives, a group that
includes snails and slugs, have a similarly

00:00:57.240 --> 00:00:58.080
bold approach.

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:03.000
Though it might not look like it, the bottom
of one of these slimy fellows is actually

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:07.440
a single, giant foot — which they slowly
ungulate to move forwards.

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:13.740
The only way this even remotely works is because
snails constantly lubricate their undersides

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:18.960
with mucus, allowing them to triumph over
friction — although just barely, as most

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:22.440
species clock in at a blazing one millimeter
per second.

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:27.420
But if your only method of locomotion was
coating your shoes in oil and shuffling your

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:29.880
feet, you probably wouldn’t get very far
either.

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:36.300
Most species in this category are *herbivores,
as anything faster than a leaf might outrun

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:36.660
them.

00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:42.300
Perhaps on another planet, a lifeform could
evolve to hop along on their one leg at greater

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:47.400
speeds — although the laws of physics mean
that any single-limbed species will likely

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:49.020
struggle with uneven terrain.

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:54.780
Still, any lineage that, when coming onto
land, looked at animals with multiple legs

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:57.660
and said ‘I’m good, thanks,’ is pretty
hardcore…

00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:04.980
Up next, we have two legs — a unique option
with some major advantages, and some major

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:08.640
disadvantages when it comes to things like…
keeping your balance.

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:14.100
The vast majority of two-legged species on
earth evolved from four-legged ancestors,

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:19.200
which means their former front limbs are now
free to be used for all kinds of weird stuff.

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:24.420
Birds are a massively successful two-legged
group whose forelimbs evolved into wings,

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:28.740
allowing them to soar through the skies in
a move that kind of feels like cheating.

00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:31.560
They barely even use their legs, it’s not
fair.

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:34.560
Not all birds take the easy route, however.

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:40.080
Ostriches and other large flightless birds
strut about with us land dwellers, and penguins,

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:40.740
well…

00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:43.380
they don’t strut, exactly, but they do their
best.

00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:48.660
Instead of wings, Penguins forelimbs have
evolved into flippers, increasing their speed

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:49.260
in the water…

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:50.940
but not so much on land.

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:56.400
But wings and flippers aren’t the only option
for what a two-legged animal does with their

00:02:56.400 --> 00:02:57.060
front limbs.

00:02:57.060 --> 00:03:03.060
Primates have evolved grabby hands they use
in all sorts of inventive ways, like snagging

00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:07.800
food, swinging from branches, and cleaning
their fur or the fur of others.

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:13.080
Now, it’s worth noting that not all Primates
are completely loyal to Team Two-Legs.

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:19.080
Great apes, for example, often switch to a
semi-four-legged posture called knuckle-walking.

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:23.580
In fact, lots of animals only switch to two
legs when it’s trendy.

00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:29.040
Bears typically plod around on all fours,
but will rear up on their hind legs to climb

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:30.540
trees or appear larger.

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:36.180
Meerkats switch to two legs when scanning
for danger, the added height giving the nervous

00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:37.500
lookouts a better view.

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:42.780
But let’s look at the true converts to the
two-leg doctrine — humans.

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:48.240
Though our stability isn’t the best at first,
an adult human’s sense of balance is on

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:48.840
another level.

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:53.940
With practice, a human’s flexibility on
two legs becomes kind of ridiculous.

00:03:53.940 --> 00:03:58.980
We might start off crawling on our hands and
feet — and like primates we can still move

00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:03.720
on all fours if we want — but it’s nowhere
near as fast as switching to a two-legged

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:05.460
stance and running full-tilt.

00:04:05.460 --> 00:04:10.620
Free from the burden of supporting our bodies,
our front limbs have become advanced tools

00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:15.000
for interacting with the world — and for
showing other animals how cool we are.

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:21.360
Across media, lots of aliens have bipedal
body plans… mainly because lots of aliens

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:23.460
are actors in budget-friendly costumes.

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:28.320
That’s not to say that two-legged life couldn’t
emerge on other worlds — they just probably

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:29.400
wouldn’t look like us.

00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:35.280
In the end, the two-leg setup is a strong
contender for the top spot… despite some

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:36.660
flaws like back pain.

00:04:38.040 --> 00:04:41.640
Next, we’ve got three legs, and right away
there’s a problem.

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:44.940
Because no species has three legs — right?

00:04:44.940 --> 00:04:47.160
Well... it’s complicated.

00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:52.920
The tripodfish is a deep-sea dweller that
perches on the sand using three ‘legs’

00:04:52.920 --> 00:04:54.900
derived from elongated fins.

00:04:54.900 --> 00:04:59.520
Whether or not these fins should be counted
as legs is debatable — as it’s actually

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:02.340
trickier to define what a leg is than you
might expect.

00:05:02.340 --> 00:05:08.400
By most scientific definitions, a leg is any
limb used especially for supporting the body

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:09.300
and for walking.

00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:15.900
Strangely enough, under this definition, Kangaroos
are another animal sometimes placed in the

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:20.640
three-leg category, because when they’re
not hopping around, their tails function as

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:22.440
third legs that keep them upright.

00:05:22.440 --> 00:05:28.440
What counts as a leg is a question that will
only get weirder as this video continues — but

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:31.500
for now, it’s clear that three legs aren’t
the most popular.

00:05:31.500 --> 00:05:36.720
Part of this may have to do with the physics
of walking, but it also could be because no

00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:41.280
early land animals came out of the water with
three limbs — and in an alternate history,

00:05:41.280 --> 00:05:44.220
evolution might have taken a stranger path.

00:05:45.660 --> 00:05:50.160
But for species afraid of breaking the mold,
four legs are a safe choice.

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:55.680
When you think land animals on planet Earth,
this is probably the number of legs that comes

00:05:55.680 --> 00:05:57.900
to mind as the default — and for good reason.

00:05:57.900 --> 00:06:03.720
Structurally, a four-leg set-up means stability
— the type of stability that lets you grow

00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:07.980
into a kind of living flagpole if evolutionary
pressures demand it.

00:06:07.980 --> 00:06:13.140
Four legs are also great for off-roading,
letting animals push through tough conditions.

00:06:13.140 --> 00:06:18.240
They allow for an ideal running gait, and
it's no accident this category is home to

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:19.800
the fastest land mammals.

00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:22.080
Really, four legs are good for whatever.

00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:27.960
But it’s not just mammals who have gotten
in on the action; reptiles have had four legs

00:06:27.960 --> 00:06:29.040
since before it was cool.

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:33.780
Another four-legged species are kangaroos
— wait, kangaroos are here too?

00:06:33.780 --> 00:06:39.120
I mean, they do walk on all fours sometimes,
but seriously — they move on two legs, three

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:40.560
legs, and four legs?

00:06:40.560 --> 00:06:41.760
Huh, I’ll allow it.

00:06:41.760 --> 00:06:42.660
I guess.

00:06:43.560 --> 00:06:49.380
The only real disadvantage to committing all
the way to four legs and no arms is that picking

00:06:49.380 --> 00:06:51.060
things up becomes a real challenge.

00:06:51.060 --> 00:06:56.160
Unless you’re an elephant, a species which
breaks all the rules by turning their snoots

00:06:56.160 --> 00:06:58.140
into long, muscular appendages.

00:06:58.140 --> 00:07:03.480
Elephants probably look at other species that
had to choose between being able to pick things

00:07:03.480 --> 00:07:05.880
up and getting really big and just laugh.

00:07:05.880 --> 00:07:10.860
An elephant’s trunk has over forty-thousand
separate muscles, more than in the entirety

00:07:10.860 --> 00:07:14.700
of the human body, which honestly feels like
they’re rubbing things in a bit.

00:07:15.240 --> 00:07:20.460
Overall, four legs are a great basic choice,
and likely would be no matter the planet.

00:07:21.480 --> 00:07:26.520
Five legs are far less popular—the category
would be pretty barren if it weren’t for

00:07:26.520 --> 00:07:28.260
a group of star-shaped oddities.

00:07:28.260 --> 00:07:33.900
Most species of starfish possess five limbs
that somewhat stretch the definition of a

00:07:33.900 --> 00:07:38.580
‘leg,’ as it’s actually the numerous
tube-like feet below each limb that allow

00:07:38.580 --> 00:07:41.040
a starfish to move along the sea floor.

00:07:41.040 --> 00:07:46.560
Their appendages are sometimes called legs,
sometimes called arms, and sometimes called

00:07:46.560 --> 00:07:52.440
‘rays’ — a third, unique category created
specially because starfish refuse to be normal.

00:07:52.440 --> 00:07:57.780
The other candidate for a five-legged animal
is the kangaroo — kangaroos again?

00:07:57.780 --> 00:08:02.160
Oh, I guess they really do sometimes use all
five — you know what, no.

00:08:02.160 --> 00:08:04.320
I don’t care that science backs them up
on this.

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:06.720
Kangaroos are officially disqualified.

00:08:07.560 --> 00:08:10.200
Moving onto six legs — just kidding.

00:08:10.200 --> 00:08:13.080
There’s another category we don’t want
to keep waiting.

00:08:13.080 --> 00:08:17.940
Though easy to overlook, because they’re
close to the ground, many animals have no

00:08:17.940 --> 00:08:18.660
legs at all.

00:08:18.660 --> 00:08:24.060
Snakes are probably the most famous group
in this category, able to move by using their

00:08:24.060 --> 00:08:26.640
muscles to push their scales against terrain.

00:08:26.640 --> 00:08:31.980
While this might sound like a huge pain, snakes
and other legless lizards gave up their appendages

00:08:31.980 --> 00:08:37.320
for good reason, as their limbless bodies
are perfectly adapted to slide into burrows

00:08:37.320 --> 00:08:39.720
and around tight corners in search of prey.

00:08:39.720 --> 00:08:45.540
Worms are also getting along just fine without
legs, moving by contracting and relaxing the

00:08:45.540 --> 00:08:46.500
segments of their body.

00:08:46.500 --> 00:08:53.220
Fish also technically qualify as legless animals,
navigating the aquatic realm with fins alone.

00:08:53.220 --> 00:08:58.680
Except for rebels like the Flying Gurnard,
which ‘walk’ along the sea floor using

00:08:58.680 --> 00:09:00.240
modified pelvic fins.

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:05.820
And some marine life like anemones and coral
can spend their whole lives in a single spot,

00:09:05.820 --> 00:09:11.280
feeding on small aquatic organisms and generally
living more like plants than animals.

00:09:11.280 --> 00:09:16.500
Many of these species could be classified
as ‘sessile,’ or fixed in one place, and

00:09:16.500 --> 00:09:18.900
should probably be in their own bonus category.

00:09:18.900 --> 00:09:23.760
It’s easy to judge, but if you could hang
out on a rock all day and have food come to

00:09:23.760 --> 00:09:26.400
you, would you really be in a hurry to go
anywhere?

00:09:28.440 --> 00:09:31.380
All right, back to six legs, for real this
time.

00:09:31.380 --> 00:09:36.840
It’s a category that might not strike you
as popular, but is actually the most widespread

00:09:36.840 --> 00:09:37.200
of all.

00:09:37.200 --> 00:09:42.960
This is because the majority of insects have
six legs, and there are around 10 quintillion

00:09:42.960 --> 00:09:48.960
insects on planet Earth, scuttling along often
unnoticed by the large animals they vastly

00:09:48.960 --> 00:09:49.620
outnumber.

00:09:49.620 --> 00:09:55.740
Six legs are incredibly versatile, enabling
insects to move while maintaining a supportive

00:09:55.740 --> 00:09:57.120
tripod at all times.

00:09:57.120 --> 00:10:02.940
Some species also evolved wings from ancient
gill-like appendages, meaning they didn’t

00:10:02.940 --> 00:10:07.080
even have to trade in a pair of limbs for
flight, which seems massively unfair.

00:10:07.080 --> 00:10:11.700
And with so many limbs to spare, some insects
have gotten pretty creative.

00:10:11.700 --> 00:10:16.500
Praying mantises are a group that have turned
their front limbs into grasping appendages

00:10:16.500 --> 00:10:20.880
while keeping their four back legs on the
ground — a body plan that’s basically

00:10:20.880 --> 00:10:23.460
as close as life on Earth gets to a centaur.

00:10:23.460 --> 00:10:29.280
There are some inventive concepts of intelligent
aliens with similar anatomy, which seems like

00:10:29.280 --> 00:10:31.500
a layout with great theoretical potential.

00:10:31.500 --> 00:10:36.840
While there are no large six-legged animals
on our planet, the unparalleled success of

00:10:36.840 --> 00:10:40.740
insects suggests that such a body plan might
have serious advantages.

00:10:42.600 --> 00:10:48.420
The Seven Leg category has almost no takers
— unless you count the Seven-Arm Octopus

00:10:48.420 --> 00:10:53.700
which keeps one of its eight tentacles used
for fertilization retracted in a sac beneath

00:10:53.700 --> 00:10:54.420
its right eye.

00:10:54.420 --> 00:10:57.360
Seems a bit like cheating, but I’ll let
it slide.

00:10:58.020 --> 00:11:03.180
Certain starfish also have seven legs, although
once again, that depends on your definition.

00:11:03.180 --> 00:11:08.220
You might have noticed that there’s a lot
of animals with an even number of legs, yet

00:11:08.220 --> 00:11:09.900
scarcely any with an odd number.

00:11:09.900 --> 00:11:15.360
This has to do with bilateral symmetry — the
tendency of animals’ right and left halves

00:11:15.360 --> 00:11:16.740
to be roughly mirror images.

00:11:16.740 --> 00:11:23.880
This trend is likely because bilateral symmetrical
species are faster and more direct than a

00:11:23.880 --> 00:11:28.740
radially symmetrical species like a starfish,
whose lack of a distinct front or back makes

00:11:28.740 --> 00:11:31.140
moving in a straight line a real challenge.

00:11:31.140 --> 00:11:36.780
The best chance for an odd-legged organism
to hit the big leagues is probably if a radially

00:11:36.780 --> 00:11:42.180
symmetrical lifeform later evolved bilateral
symmetry, which seems to be the origin of

00:11:42.180 --> 00:11:43.980
the seven-limbed aliens in Arrival.

00:11:45.180 --> 00:11:51.180
Back to even numbers, eight legs are the domain
of insects’ cool older siblings — the

00:11:51.180 --> 00:11:52.260
arachnids.

00:11:52.260 --> 00:11:57.360
The poster children of this group are spiders
and tarantulas, most of whom have turned their

00:11:57.360 --> 00:12:00.300
eight legs into all-terrain web climbing machines.

00:12:00.300 --> 00:12:05.640
Some people find an arachnid’s legs overkill,
but they might look at us and think we’re

00:12:05.640 --> 00:12:07.080
seriously underequipped.

00:12:07.740 --> 00:12:13.380
The other most famous eight-legged contender
is the octopus, who typically possess eight

00:12:13.380 --> 00:12:17.940
multi-purpose tentacles that can both carry
them across the sea floor and function as

00:12:17.940 --> 00:12:18.420
arms.

00:12:18.420 --> 00:12:24.240
Some have argued that the octopus is technically
a biped, as they’ll sometimes use only two

00:12:24.240 --> 00:12:26.220
tentacles to move across the sand.

00:12:26.220 --> 00:12:31.200
This depends on the species, however, and
there’s a strong case to be made that an

00:12:31.200 --> 00:12:34.980
octopuses’ tentacles meet the criteria for
both arms and legs.

00:12:34.980 --> 00:12:39.720
Proving that you can’t put an octopus in
a box; they’ll just ooze back out again.

00:12:40.380 --> 00:12:45.900
Both octopuses and spiders have been observed
functioning just fine with missing limbs—because

00:12:45.900 --> 00:12:48.720
when you have eight legs, you’ve got enough
to spare.

00:12:50.040 --> 00:12:56.220
The nine-leg category is another unpopular
odd-number section, with the closest contenders

00:12:56.220 --> 00:13:02.280
being the Nine-Rayed Starfish, or the exceedingly
rare nine-armed octopus — although its extra

00:13:02.280 --> 00:13:05.760
limb only happens when a severed tentacle
regrows as two.

00:13:06.660 --> 00:13:12.000
But with ten legs, things really get crazy
as we enter the domain of crustaceans.

00:13:12.000 --> 00:13:17.820
Many species of lobster and shrimp have a
full ten legs, and with that many legs available,

00:13:17.820 --> 00:13:19.500
things can get weird.

00:13:19.500 --> 00:13:24.600
Crabs are crustaceans that have gone the centaur
route and lifted their front limbs off the

00:13:24.600 --> 00:13:25.980
ground to use as a pinchers.

00:13:26.520 --> 00:13:32.040
Some crabs have even turned their back legs
into flippers to, uh, ‘swim’ — because

00:13:32.040 --> 00:13:34.920
when you have ten limbs to work with, you
might as well.

00:13:34.920 --> 00:13:41.040
And as we know, the crab model is a favorite
of evolution, as numerous other crustaceans

00:13:41.040 --> 00:13:46.920
have converted to the way of the crab — creating
multiple ‘false crab’ lineages that jealously

00:13:46.920 --> 00:13:48.960
copy the crabs’ winning formula.

00:13:49.500 --> 00:13:54.780
While more legs aren’t always better, the
stunning popularity of crustaceans suggests

00:13:54.780 --> 00:13:58.860
that they’re onto something with their high
limb-count — and that life on other planets

00:13:58.860 --> 00:14:00.360
might just be a bunch of crabs.

00:14:01.980 --> 00:14:08.160
Beyond the event horizon of ten legs, we enter
a black hole of ever-increasing digits.

00:14:08.160 --> 00:14:12.720
Giant, deep-sea isopods have a staggering
fourteen legs.

00:14:12.720 --> 00:14:18.480
Caterpillars have a rough average of sixteen
total legs — with six primary limbs in the

00:14:18.480 --> 00:14:23.520
front and multiple rows of pseudo-feet to
help them move their elongated bodies.

00:14:24.120 --> 00:14:29.880
Centipedes can have anywhere from sixteen
to over a hundred legs, and finally, some

00:14:29.880 --> 00:14:32.820
millipedes can have an even higher number
than that.

00:14:33.600 --> 00:14:38.880
And the species with the absolute highest
number of legs is the Eumillipes persephone

00:14:38.880 --> 00:14:43.920
which has an astonishing one-thousand and
three-hundred legs in total.

00:14:43.920 --> 00:14:49.260
Though an impressive total count, such a number
is only needed to help the species push through

00:14:49.260 --> 00:14:55.200
the soil deep below ground, and would be an
absurdly impractical number for the vast majority

00:14:55.200 --> 00:14:55.800
of life

00:14:55.800 --> 00:14:56.300
on earth.

00:14:58.560 --> 00:15:04.200
So, what’s the Answer? Well, it depends.&nbsp;
And yes, I know that’s not the most fun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.200 --> 00:15:08.760
answer — but I promise it’s the one that’s&nbsp;
accurate. There are some stand outs, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:08.760 --> 00:15:14.940
as easy as it would be to arbitrarily assign two&nbsp;
legs, or six legs, or ten legs as the winner — it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:14.940 --> 00:15:19.320
would be ignoring the complexity of nature.&nbsp;
If there is one thing we’ve learned, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:19.320 --> 00:15:24.180
it’s that nature shows an astonishing amount&nbsp;
of variability when it comes to leg numbers;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:25.020 --> 00:15:30.720
should you ever design a lifeform, know you have&nbsp;
a lot of creative freedom in terms of total limbs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:31.260 --> 00:15:37.800
In the end, we should strive to appreciate the&nbsp;
leg counts of all animals. …Except for kangaroos.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:39.180 --> 00:15:43.980
And speaking of numbers, I’d like to thank&nbsp;
all my subscribers for bringing Curious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:43.980 --> 00:15:48.720
Archive past the five-hundred thousand&nbsp;
milestone, plus a little extra. That’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.720 --> 00:15:53.700
over four-hundred and ninety-eight thousand&nbsp;
